# Records - Field Decisions

**Purpose:** Decisions about the field API in `@art-js/records` — naming, resolution, rename, form selection, the string-write route, and the read model.

## Decision: Canonical Field Names

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** Fields are addressed by field name — one address space, no label fallback, no dual mode. A canonical field name matches `/^[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9]*$/`, and only names supplied through the API must be canonical; API-created fields carry the derived label of their name. Extracted names come from the stored label as-is: never rejected for failing the grammar, never rewritten by a value update. A stored label does not round-trip by design; the only required round trip is name → derived label → name. There is no label escape hatch — the raw section is the only route to non-addressable fields. The API provides separate predicates for syntax and for reservation; they compose rather than overlap.

## Decision: The name → field Index

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** Extraction builds one name → field index per record in document order; the first occurrence of a name wins and later duplicates stay in the section, shadowed. Every access resolves through the index — no access path scans section fields or converts a label. Removing the indexed field, by delete or by a rename that vacates the name, promotes the next duplicate, which keeps its own stored label. A label that resolves to a reserved name aborts construction, so the document cannot be extracted until the label is fixed. Access guards follow the name classes: reserved names reject every operation, the canonical grammar gates creation only, and a delete that removes nothing reports failure.

## Decision: Rename by Name

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** Renaming operates on names, never labels, and validates before mutating anything: both names are checked for reservation, the source must exist, and the target must be canonical and unoccupied — in that order, with the collision check last so a rejected rename leaves the record untouched. On success the field is relabelled to the derived label of the target name, its position is preserved, and the old spelling is forgotten. The source name is not held to the canonical grammar — it may be any extracted name — while the target must be. Vacating the source name promotes a shadowed duplicate if one exists. Relabelling is the one operation that rewrites a stored label.

## Decision: Automatic Field Form

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** Whether a field is inline or block is chosen from the value being set — the caller never selects it, and no overload, option, or method takes a form. Content that is empty or purely phrasing, including a single parsed paragraph, becomes inline; any block-level content becomes block. Writing to an existing field replaces its value and converts its form in place when the classification changes, preserving name, tags, and position — so a phrasing-only value makes a block field inline, and keeping it block-shaped means carrying block content. `Document`, field, and section containers are rejected as values, so neither a nested field nor a whole document can ever become a field's value.

## Decision: Strings Parse as Markdown

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** Every string input is parsed as markdown rather than stored as a literal text node, so markdown forms survive set → save → read. Numbers and booleans are coerced to string and take the same route, preserving scalar writes; any other non-string value is rejected. The API provides separate methods for the string route and for the value route, and the proxy accepts both. Text containing markdown characters must be escaped or passed as a value. One string mechanism, so the same string behaves identically through either route.

## Decision: Reads Split Into Markdown, Text, and Raw Value

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** The API provides three separate reads of a field value: markdown, as the default access; text, preserving the previous markup-stripped rendering; and the raw value as a deep clone of the field's value — a clone, never the live object, so a consumer cannot desynchronise the record by mutating what it read. Consumers that displayed the default read see markdown markers after the change and move to the text read for the previous behaviour. The expanded accessors are reserved names, so the set of document labels that abort extraction grows accordingly.
